    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda CRF1150L Africa Twin Enduro Sports Is a Tongue Twister, Weighs Less but Offers More\nThe UK's Renault Twingo EV Retails From £14,745 After Full £3,750 Electric Car Grant\n169 photos\nPhoto: Renault\nRenault has managed to price its cutesy Twingo E-Tech from £14,745 in the United Kingdom, and the French automaker hasn't achieved that figure by ripping out features that come in handy on a daily basis. A proper automobile instead of a glorified mobility scooter, the small hatch can be this affordable by folding the £3,750 electric vehicle grant into the advertised price.\nRemove it, and the base Evolution grade would otherwise cost £18,495 before optional extras. A decidedly less interesting number, for sure. In the United Kingdom, the Twingo E-Tech gets a front-mounted electric drive unit with close to 80 ponies to its name, along with a 27.5-kWh battery.\nA lithium-iron-phosphate battery, that is, for the nickel-manganese-cobalt alternative would be too expensive for a low-cost vehicle. In the WLTP, the French automaker's British division expects no more than 163 miles (262 kilometers) of driving on a full charge of said LFP battery.\nMeasuring just under 3.8 meters, which is nearly 150 inches over in the United States (where Renault packed up and left in 1987), the zero-emission hatchback measures 2,493 millimeters (98.1 inches) between its front and rear axles. Not bad for such a small vehicle, especially when you remember that Ford's discontinued Fiesta used to measure 2,493 millimeters in wheelbase as well.\nGifted with playful styling and perfectly so proportions, the retro-tastic hatchback won't be mistaken for any other cheap battery-electric vehicle on the road. Its cheerful personality carries over inside, where four occupants can squeeze in. The rear seats can be folded independently, and they also slide for good measure.\nPhoto: Renault Both solutions allow the driver to strike a balance between passenger and cargo space without sacrificing the entire back row, and this versatility is exactly what you'd expect from a small European hatchback. The Renault UK configurator lists anything from 305 liters at minimum and up to 1,010 liters (35.7 cubic feet) tops with the rear seats folded and slid in the most cargo-favoring position.\nRated at 175 Newton-meters (just under 130 pound-feet) of twist, the Twingo E-Tech needs a leisurely 12.1 seconds to hit 62 miles per hour (100 kilometers per hour). Top speed? An honest 130 kilometers per hour (nearly 81 miles per hour), which means that highway driving at Europe's posted top speeds of 70 mph in the UK and 130 kph in many continental regions would max it out.\nCharging, however, is a compromise of sorts. Renault may have included an 11-kW AC charger, but fast charging on DC tops 50 kilowatts. The battery's capacity negates the poor DC performance, though. In optimal conditions, you need half an hour to recharge the LFP battery pack from 10 to 80 percent.\nEvolution customers get 16-inch steel wheels by default, along with decorative covers for them, a 7-inch display for the driver, 10-inch touchscreen infotainment, manual air conditioning, cruise control, lane-keeping assist, rear parking sensors, traffic sign recognition, and automatic emergency braking. The better-equipped Techno adds larger alloys, automatic climate control, a reversing camera, heated front seats, Google built-in services, one-pedal driving, and a hands-free key card.\nStepping up to the Launch Edition, prospective customers are presented with rear cross-traffic alert, hands-free parking, blind-spot warning, an interior equipment pack, and free-of-charge paint options. Those would be Mango Yellow and Absolute Green, the latter being my favorite shade available this model year.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nRenault Twingo E-Tech Renault Twingo Renault UK price EV\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Owner Spent $30,000 To Restore This Chevy Bel Air, Now Selling for $33,500",
